.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-CHANGE-EMAIL 1 "November 2026" "euclid-cli 2.1.3" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-change-email \- give a user a different email address
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eam change-email
.BR \-\-user | \-u
.I USERID
.BR \-\-email | \-e
.I ADDRESS
.SH DESCRIPTION
Gives a user a different email address. Requires administrator
privileges.
.PP
An address is a way in, not just a detail recorded about a user:
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
accepts one in place of a user ID. That is why this command always names
the user it changes, rather than meaning "me" when it names nobody.
.PP
It is refused when another user already holds the address. Addresses are
unique across the installation, since two users sharing one would make
login ambiguous.
.PP
Nothing else about the user changes: the user ID, the ERN built from it,
their grants, group memberships and access keys all stay as they were. A
session already open keeps working. The next login has to use the new
address, or the user ID, which keeps working throughout.
.PP
An address cannot be cleared here. An empty one is refused rather than
stored.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSERID\fR
User whose address is changing. Required.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-email " " \fIADDRESS\fR
The address they should have from now on. Required, and not empty.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The address was changed.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, the caller
lacks administrator privileges, no such user exists, or another user
already holds the address.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eam change-email --user jdoe --email jane.doe@example.com
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-get-user (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-change-userid (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
